CAT = "crfscCare"; SEG = "CAR"
Q = [
("Where should you look for the steps to field strip a particular semi-automatic pistol?",
 "The maker's manual", ["Any pistol's manual", "The label on the box", "The registration certificate"],
 "Takedown steps differ from model to model. Follow the maker's manual and do not take a handgun apart further than it describes."),
("On a revolver, where does a dark ring of carbon and lead usually build up?",
 "On the cylinder's front face", ["Inside the grip frame", "Under the rear sight", "Along the face of the trigger"],
 "Hot gas escaping at the cylinder gap leaves carbon and lead on the front face of the cylinder and around the forcing cone."),
("What is leading in a handgun barrel?",
 "Lead deposits in the rifling", ["Rust inside the cylinder chambers", "Oil left in the bore", "A dent at the muzzle"],
 "Plain lead bullets can leave streaks of lead in the grooves. Heavy leading hurts accuracy and should be scrubbed out with a suitable brush and solvent."),
("When cleaning a revolver, which parts besides the barrel need a brush?",
 "Each chamber of the cylinder", ["The grip panels only", "The stamped serial number area", "The front sight blade"],
 "Every chamber collects fouling and must be cleaned as well as the bore, or cases can stick and become hard to eject."),
("Why should you protect the muzzle crown when cleaning a handgun from the front?",
 "Damage there hurts accuracy", ["It stops the barrel unscrewing", "It keeps solvent off the grip", "It makes the rod shorter"],
 "The crown is the last surface the bullet touches. A rod rubbing on it can wear it unevenly, so use a guide or keep the rod centred."),
("What should you check on a pistol's magazine during cleaning?",
 "The feed lips and spring", ["The serial number", "The paint colour", "The logo on the baseplate"],
 "Bent feed lips or a weak spring cause feeding problems. Keep magazines clean and dry and replace damaged ones."),
("Why should a striker-fired pistol's striker channel usually be kept free of oil?",
 "Oil gathers dirt and slows it", ["Oil makes it fire twice", "Oil changes the calibre", "Oil seizes the slide shut in heat"],
 "Many makers warn against oiling the striker channel because oil gathers grit that can slow the striker and cause light strikes. Follow the manual."),
("You find a crack in a revolver's forcing cone during cleaning. What should you do?",
 "Stop using it, see a gunsmith", ["Shoot lighter loads only", "Fill the crack with oil or grease", "Sand it smooth by hand"],
 "A cracked forcing cone can fail under pressure. Take the revolver out of use until a qualified gunsmith has inspected or repaired it."),
("Why should a pistol's recoil spring be checked and replaced as the maker advises?",
 "A worn spring affects cycling", ["It sets the weight of the trigger", "It holds the sights on", "It seals the chamber"],
 "The recoil spring returns the slide. When it weakens, the slide can batter the frame or fail to feed reliably."),
("After cleaning a pistol and putting it back together, what should you do before storing it?",
 "Function check it unloaded", ["Leave the hammer cocked", "Leave the magazine in", "Store it with the slide forward on a round"],
 "With the handgun unloaded and pointed safely, check that the slide, safety, slide stop and trigger work as they should, then store it lawfully."),
("Why should you not apply heavy grease inside a handgun's action?",
 "It traps grit and slows parts", ["It changes the classification", "It makes the barrel rust", "It stops rifling wear"],
 "A light film of the right lubricant is enough. Thick grease collects dirt and can stiffen moving parts, especially in cold weather."),
("What is a bore snake?",
 "A pull-through cleaning cord", ["A stiff, one-piece steel rod", "A bright chamber flag", "A vented muzzle brake"],
 "A bore snake is a flexible cord with a brush section that is pulled through the barrel, a quick way to remove light fouling."),
("A handgun you bought used seems to work but shows heavy rust and loose parts. Before firing it, you should:",
 "Have a gunsmith inspect it", ["Fire a full cylinder first", "Oil it and try it", "Tighten every screw"],
 "Rust and wear can hide cracks or timing problems. A qualified gunsmith should check it before it is fired."),
("Which revolver fault means the cylinder does not lock each chamber in line with the barrel?",
 "Poor timing", ["Light strikes", "Leading", "Pitting rust"],
 "Timing is how the hand and cylinder stop index each chamber with the bore. A badly timed revolver can shave lead and must be repaired."),
("When you clean a handgun that has been fired with corrosive surplus ammunition, what matters most?",
 "Clean it soon with a salt remover", ["Wait a week before cleaning", "Use only dry patches", "Oil it heavily without cleaning"],
 "Corrosive primer salts draw moisture and cause rust quickly. Clean the bore and action soon after shooting with a cleaner that dissolves the salts, then dry and oil."),
("Why should handgun grips be checked during routine care?",
 "Loose grips hurt control", ["They hold the barrel on", "They set the sight height", "They mark the calibre"],
 "Grips that are loose or cracked shift in the hand, which hurts control. Tighten or replace them as the maker advises."),
]
